// ────────────── Output Configuration ──────────────
/*
You can configure outputs by their name, which you can find
by running `niri msg outputs` while inside a niri instance.
The built-in laptop monitor is usually called "eDP-1".
Find more information on the wiki:
https://niri-wm.github.io/niri/Configuration:-Outputs
Remember to uncomment the node by removing "/-"!

NOTE: This file is included AFTER dms/outputs.kdl in config.kdl, so it
intentionally OVERRIDES DMS's auto-generated output block. DMS's auto-detect
picks 3440x1440@165.001 + UNCONDITIONAL (always-on) VRR on this monitor,
which caused intermittent striped/corrupted display + luminosity flicker
after login (2026-08-24, recurred 2026-08-25).

165Hz IS the monitor's real native mode (confirmed via journal:
`niri::backend::tty: picking mode: ... vrefresh: 165, mode_type:
ModeTypeFlags(PREFERRED | DRIVER)`, clean clock 878530 — not an
interpolated/synthetic timing) — never the trigger, no reason to avoid it.

VRR IS the trigger — this monitor exhibits luminosity/brightness flicker
under VRR (backlight duty-cycle modulation as frame timing varies; common
FreeSync behavior on some panels, not a driver bug). `on-demand=true` was
tried first (VRR only for windows with `variable-refresh-rate true`) but
proved unreliable: live checks showed it silently reverting to
unconditional-enabled at idle with zero windows requesting VRR and no
config/file change to explain it — root cause unresolved, not worth
chasing further. VRR is now **off entirely** (2026-08-25) — confirmed live
via `niri msg output DP-1 vrr off` that flicker stops immediately. Tradeoff
accepted: no more VRR smoothing for fullscreen games (tearing/stutter can
return under niri's compositor fullscreen) — see steam_app window-rule in
config.kdl, its `variable-refresh-rate true` is now a no-op since the
output doesn't support enabling it per-window without VRR itself being on.

Editing dms/outputs.kdl directly does NOT work as a fix: DMS overwrites it
within seconds of every session start regardless of content, so any
override has to live here instead, in a git-tracked file DMS never
touches.
*/

output "DP-1" {

	/* Uncomment this line to disable this output. */
	// off

	/*
	Resolution and, optionally, refresh rate of the output.
	The format is "<width>x<height>" or "<width>x<height>@<refresh rate>".
	If the refresh rate is omitted, niri will pick the highest refresh rate
	for the resolution.
	If the mode is omitted altogether or is invalid, niri will pick one automatically.
	Run `niri msg outputs` while inside a niri instance to list all outputs and their modes.
	*/
	mode "3440x1440@165"

	/* You can use integer or fractional scale, for example use 1.5 for 150% scale. */
	scale 1

	/*
	Variable refresh rate (FreeSync/G-Sync) — DELIBERATELY OMITTED (= off).
	This monitor flickers (luminosity/brightness) under VRR, even on-demand.
	See file header note above (2026-08-25) before re-enabling.
	*/

	/*
	Transform allows to rotate the output counter-clockwise, valid values are:
	normal, 90, 180, 270, flipped, flipped-90, flipped-180 and flipped-270.
	*/
	transform "normal"

	/*
	Position of the output in the global coordinate space.
	This affects directional monitor actions like "focus-monitor-left", and cursor movement.
	The cursor can only move between directly adjacent outputs.
	Output scale and rotation has to be taken into account for positioning:
	outputs are sized in logical, or scaled, pixels.
	For example, a 3840×2160 output with scale 2.0 will have a logical size of 1920×1080,
	so to put another output directly adjacent to it on the right, set its x to 1920.
	If the position is unset or results in an overlap, the output is instead placed
	automatically.
	*/
    position x=0 y=0
}
